      "side": 1,
      "message": "`if` and `foreach` are two special cases of constructs that can be either a node or an expression. So they appear in both `knownFunctions` and `knownNodeFunctions`. We will prefer the node version of them if possible. This is so that if someone writes:\n\n```\n$(foreach module,\\\n  $(BOOT_KERNEL_MODULES_LIST),\\\n  $(if $(filter $(module),foo.ko),,$(error module \"$(module)\" has an error!)))\n```\n\nwe get:\n\n```\nfor module in g[\"BOOT_KERNEL_MODULES_LIST\"]:\n  if not rblf.filter(module, \"foo.ko\"):\n    rblf.mkerror(\"product.mk\", \"module \\\"%s\\\" has an error!\" % module)\n```\n\ninstead of:\n\n```\n[\"\" if rblf.filter(module, \"foo.ko\") else rblf.mkerror(\"product.mk\", \"module \\\"%s\\\" has an error!\" % module) for module in g[\"BOOT_KERNEL_MODULES_LIST\"]]\n```\n\nNote that we will still get the expression version of the above if it was used in an expression context, like an argument to a function call.\n\nFor `$(eval)`, `$(call inherit-product)`, and `$(call inherit-product-if-exists)`, I would argue those are definitely nodes, not expressions, as they return an empty string every time. It also wouldn\u0027t make sense to put an assignment from a eval in the middle of an expression. (ex: `\"foo %s bar\" % (PRODUCT_PACKAGES +\u003d [\"baz\"])` doesn\u0027t make any sense)",
